{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: days 1–10 are close (small errors, often within ±0.5), but from ~day 15 onward the model increasingly underestimates when the market trends up (misses late-rally acceleration by ~+3 to +9 by days 25–30). Apply an upward drift adjustment that increases with horizon when recent momentum is positive.",
    "Trend/acceleration miss: the model tends to project sideways-to-gentle mean reversion (~66–70) even when the realized path transitions into a strong breakout (true series climbs into 71–76). Add a breakout regime rule: if last 5–10 days show higher highs and higher lows, increase slope/convexity of the forecast tail (days 20–30).",
    "Over-mean-reversion after dips: around mid-horizon drawdowns (true dips to ~64–66), the model often tracks the dip but then fails to re-accelerate with the subsequent rebound. Reduce mean-reversion strength beyond ~day 15; allow recovery to overshoot prior range when momentum resumes.",
    "Lag in responding to late shocks: large late-horizon upside moves are incorporated too slowly (model stays near prior range while truth jumps). Use a volatility/momentum trigger: if recent realized volatility or 3–5 day return exceeds a threshold, widen forecast distribution and shift the mean upward for longer horizons.",
    "Ceiling anchoring: forecasts appear implicitly capped near ~70, causing systematic underprediction once prices enter a higher regime. Remove/relax any soft cap by allowing the forecast to follow recent trend until a clear reversal signal appears."
  ]
}
